<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     This past year our registered shareholders increased from=20
about 1900 to about 2900.  Most of this growth resulted from our=20
merger with Blue Chip Stamps, but there also was an acceleration=20
in the pace of =93natural=94 increase that has raised us from the=20
1000 level a few years ago.

     With so many new shareholders, it=92s appropriate to summarize=20
the major business principles we follow that pertain to the=20
manager-owner relationship:

   <b>o</b> Although our form is corporate, our attitude is=20
partnership.  Charlie Munger and I think of our shareholders as=20
owner-partners, and of ourselves as managing partners.  (Because=20
of the size of our shareholdings we also are, for better or=20
worse, controlling partners.) We do not view the company itself=20
as the ultimate owner of our business assets but, instead, view=20
the company as a conduit through which our shareholders own the=20
assets.

   <b>o</b> In line with this owner-orientation, our directors are all=20
major shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway.  In the case of at=20
least four of the five, over 50% of family net worth is=20
represented by holdings of Berkshire.  We eat our own cooking.

   <b>o</b> Our long-term economic goal (subject to some qualifications=20
mentioned later) is to maximize the average annual rate of gain=20
in intrinsic business value on a per-share basis.  We do not=20
measure the economic significance or performance of Berkshire by=20
its size; we measure by per-share progress.  We are certain that=20
the rate of per-share progress will diminish in the future - a=20
greatly enlarged capital base will see to that.  But we will be=20
disappointed if our rate does not exceed that of the average=20
large American corporation.

   <b>o</b> Our preference would be to reach this goal by directly=20
owning a diversified group of businesses that generate cash and=20
consistently earn above-average returns on capital.  Our second=20
choice is to own parts of similar businesses, attained primarily=20
through purchases of marketable common stocks by our insurance=20
subsidiaries.  The price and availability of businesses and the=20
need for insurance capital determine any given year=92s capital=20
allocation.

   <b>o</b> Because of this two-pronged approach to business ownership=20
and because of the limitations of conventional accounting,=20
consolidated reported earnings may reveal relatively little about=20
our true economic performance.  Charlie and I, both as owners and=20
managers, virtually ignore such consolidated numbers.  However,=20
we will also report to you the earnings of each major business we=20
control, numbers we consider of great importance.  These figures,=20
along with other information we will supply about the individual=20
businesses, should generally aid you in making judgments about=20
them.

   <b>o</b> Accounting consequences do not influence our operating or=20
capital-allocation decisions.  When acquisition costs are=20
similar, we much prefer to purchase $2 of earnings that is not=20
reportable by us under standard accounting principles than to=20
purchase $1 of earnings that is reportable.  This is precisely=20
the choice that often faces us since entire businesses (whose=20
earnings will be fully reportable) frequently sell for double the=20
pro-rata price of small portions (whose earnings will be largely=20
unreportable).  In aggregate and over time, we expect the=20
unreported earnings to be fully reflected in our intrinsic=20
business value through capital gains.

   <b>o</b> We rarely use much debt and, when we do, we attempt to=20
structure it on a long-term fixed rate basis.  We will reject=20
interesting opportunities rather than over-leverage our balance=20
sheet.  This conservatism has penalized our results but it is the=20
only behavior that leaves us comfortable, considering our=20
fiduciary obligations to policyholders, depositors, lenders and=20
the many equity holders who have committed unusually large=20
portions of their net worth to our care.

   <b>o</b> A managerial =93wish list=94 will not be filled at shareholder=
=20
expense.  We will not diversify by purchasing entire businesses=20
at control prices that ignore long-term economic consequences to=20
our shareholders.  We will only do with your money what we would=20
do with our own, weighing fully the values you can obtain by=20
diversifying your own portfolios through direct purchases in the=20
stock market.

   <b>o</b> We feel noble intentions should be checked periodically=20
against results.  We test the wisdom of retaining earnings by=20
assessing whether retention, over time, delivers shareholders at=20
least $1 of market value for each $1 retained.  To date, this=20
test has been met.  We will continue to apply it on a five-year=20
rolling basis.  As our net worth grows, it is more difficult to=20
use retained earnings wisely.

   <b>o</b> We will issue common stock only when we receive as much in=20
business value as we give.  This rule applies to all forms of=20
issuance - not only mergers or public stock offerings, but stock=20
for-debt swaps, stock options, and convertible securities as=20
well.  We will not sell small portions of your company - and that=20
is what the issuance of shares amounts to - on a basis=20
inconsistent with the value of the entire enterprise.

   <b>o</b> You should be fully aware of one attitude Charlie and I=20
share that hurts our financial performance: regardless of price,=20
we have no interest at all in selling any good businesses that=20
Berkshire owns, and are very reluctant to sell sub-par businesses=20
as long as we expect them to generate at least some cash and as=20
long as we feel good about their managers and labor relations. =20
We hope not to repeat the capital-allocation mistakes that led us=20
into such sub-par businesses.  And we react with great caution to=20
suggestions that our poor businesses can be restored to=20
satisfactory profitability by major capital expenditures.  (The=20
projections will be dazzling - the advocates will be sincere -=20
but, in the end, major additional investment in a terrible=20
industry usually is about as rewarding as struggling in=20
quicksand.) Nevertheless, gin rummy managerial behavior (discard=20
your least promising business at each turn) is not our style.  We=20
would rather have our overall results penalized a bit than engage=20
in it.

   <b>o</b> We will be candid in our reporting to you, emphasizing the=20
pluses and minuses important in appraising business value.  Our=20
guideline is to tell you the business facts that we would want to=20
know if our positions were reversed.  We owe you no less. =20
Moreover, as a company with a major communications business, it=20
would be inexcusable for us to apply lesser standards of=20
accuracy, balance and incisiveness when reporting on ourselves=20
than we would expect our news people to apply when reporting on=20
others.  We also believe candor benefits us as managers: the CEO=20
who misleads others in public may eventually mislead himself in=20
private.

   <b>o</b> Despite our policy of candor, we will discuss our=20
activities in marketable securities only to the extent legally=20
required.  Good investment ideas are rare, valuable and subject=20
to competitive appropriation just as good product or business=20
acquisition ideas are.  Therefore, we normally will not talk=20
about our investment ideas.  This ban extends even to securities=20
we have sold (because we may purchase them again) and to stocks=20
we are incorrectly rumored to be buying.  If we deny those=20
reports but say =93no comment=94 on other occasions, the no-comments=20
become confirmation.

     That completes the catechism, and we can now move on to the=20
high point of 1983 - the acquisition of a majority interest in=20
Nebraska Furniture Mart and our association with Rose Blumkin and=20
her family.

<b>Nebraska Furniture Mart</b>

     Last year, in discussing how managers with bright, but=20
adrenalin-soaked minds scramble after foolish acquisitions, I=20
quoted Pascal: =93It has struck me that all the misfortunes of men=20
spring from the single cause that they are unable to stay quietly=20
in one room.=94

     Even Pascal would have left the room for Mrs. Blumkin.

     About 67 years ago Mrs. Blumkin, then 23, talked her way=20
past a border guard to leave Russia for America.  She had no=20
formal education, not even at the grammar school level, and knew=20
no English.  After some years in this country, she learned the=20
language when her older daughter taught her, every evening, the=20
words she had learned in school during the day.

     In 1937, after many years of selling used clothing, Mrs. =20
Blumkin had saved $500 with which to realize her dream of opening=20
a furniture store.  Upon seeing the American Furniture Mart in=20
Chicago - then the center of the nation=92s wholesale furniture=20
activity - she decided to christen her dream Nebraska Furniture=20
Mart.

     She met every obstacle you would expect (and a few you=20
wouldn=92t) when a business endowed with only $500 and no=20
locational or product advantage goes up against rich, long-
entrenched competition.  At one early point, when her tiny=20
resources ran out, =93Mrs.  B=94 (a personal trademark now as well=20
recognized in Greater Omaha as Coca-Cola or Sanka) coped in a way=20
not taught at business schools: she simply sold the furniture and=20
appliances from her home in order to pay creditors precisely as=20
promised.

     Omaha retailers began to recognize that Mrs. B would offer=20
customers far better deals than they had been giving, and they=20
pressured furniture and carpet manufacturers not to sell to her. =20
But by various strategies she obtained merchandise and cut prices=20
sharply.  Mrs. B was then hauled into court for violation of Fair=20
Trade laws.  She not only won all the cases, but received=20
invaluable publicity.  At the end of one case, after=20
demonstrating to the court that she could profitably sell carpet=20
at a huge discount from the prevailing price, she sold the judge=20
$1400 worth of carpet.

     Today Nebraska Furniture Mart generates over $100 million of=20
sales annually out of one 200,000 square-foot store.  No other=20
home furnishings store in the country comes close to that volume. =20
That single store also sells more furniture, carpets, and=20
appliances than do all Omaha competitors combined.

     One question I always ask myself in appraising a business is=20
how I would like, assuming I had ample capital and skilled=20
personnel, to compete with it.  I=92d rather wrestle grizzlies than=20
compete with Mrs. B and her progeny.  They buy brilliantly, they=20
operate at expense ratios competitors don=92t even dream about, and=20
they then pass on to their customers much of the savings.  It=92s=20
the ideal business - one built upon exceptional value to the=20
customer that in turn translates into exceptional economics for=20
its owners.

     Mrs. B is wise as well as smart and, for far-sighted family=20
reasons, was willing to sell the business last year.  I had=20
admired both the family and the business for decades, and a deal=20
was quickly made.  But Mrs. B, now 90, is not one to go home and=20
risk, as she puts it, =93losing her marbles=94.  She remains Chairman=20
and is on the sales floor seven days a week.  Carpet sales are=20
her specialty.  She personally sells quantities that would be a=20
good departmental total for other carpet retailers.

     We purchased 90% of the business - leaving 10% with members=20
of the family who are involved in management - and have optioned=20
10% to certain key young family managers.

     And what managers they are.  Geneticists should do=20
handsprings over the Blumkin family.  Louie Blumkin, Mrs.  B=92s=20
son, has been President of Nebraska Furniture Mart for many years=20
and is widely regarded as the shrewdest buyer of furniture and=20
appliances in the country.  Louie says he had the best teacher,=20
and Mrs. B says she had the best student.  They=92re both right. =20
Louie and his three sons all have the Blumkin business ability,=20
work ethic, and, most important, character.  On top of that, they=20
are really nice people.  We are delighted to be in partnership=20
with them.

<b>Corporate Performance</b>

     During 1983 our book value increased from $737.43 per share=20
to $975.83 per share, or by 32%.  We never take the one-year=20
figure very seriously.  After all, why should the time required=20
for a planet to circle the sun synchronize precisely with the=20
time required for business actions to pay off?  Instead, we=20
recommend not less than a five-year test as a rough yardstick of=20
economic performance.  Red lights should start flashing if the=20
five-year average annual gain falls much below the return on=20
equity earned over the period by American industry in aggregate.=20
(Watch out for our explanation if that occurs as Goethe observed,=20
=93When ideas fail, words come in very handy.=94)

     During the 19-year tenure of present management, book value=20
has grown from $19.46 per share to $975.83, or 22.6% compounded=20
annually.  Considering our present size, nothing close to this=20
rate of return can be sustained.  Those who believe otherwise=20
should pursue a career in sales, but avoid one in mathematics.

     We report our progress in terms of book value because in our=20
case (though not, by any means, in all cases) it is a=20
conservative but reasonably adequate proxy for growth in=20
intrinsic business value - <i>the measurement that really counts.</i> =20
Book value=92s virtue as a score-keeping measure is that it is easy=20
to calculate and doesn=92t involve the subjective (but important)=20
judgments employed in calculation of intrinsic business value. =20
It is important to understand, however, that the two terms - book=20
value and intrinsic business value - have very different=20
meanings.

     Book value is an accounting concept, recording the=20
accumulated financial input from both contributed capital and=20
retained earnings.  Intrinsic business value is an economic=20
concept, estimating future cash output discounted to present=20
value.  Book value tells you what has been put in; intrinsic=20
business value estimates what can be taken out.

     An analogy will suggest the difference.  Assume you spend=20
identical amounts putting each of two children through college. =20
The book value (measured by financial input) of each child=92s=20
education would be the same.  But the present value of the future=20
payoff (the intrinsic business value) might vary enormously -=20
from zero to many times the cost of the education.  So, also, do=20
businesses having equal financial input end up with wide=20
variations in value.

     At Berkshire, at the beginning of fiscal 1965 when the=20
present management took over, the $19.46 per share book value=20
considerably overstated intrinsic business value.  All of that=20
book value consisted of textile assets that could not earn, on=20
average, anything close to an appropriate rate of return.  In the=20
terms of our analogy, the investment in textile assets resembled=20
investment in a largely-wasted education.

     Now, however, our intrinsic business value considerably=20
exceeds book value.  There are two major reasons:

     (1) Standard accounting principles require that common=20
         stocks held by our insurance subsidiaries be stated on=20
         our books at market value, but that other stocks we own=20
         be carried at the lower of aggregate cost or market. =20
         At the end of 1983, the market value of this latter=20
         group exceeded carrying value by $70 million pre-tax,=20
         or about $50 million after tax.  This excess belongs in=20
         our intrinsic business value, but is not included in=20
         the calculation of book value;

     (2) More important, we own several businesses that possess=20
         economic Goodwill (which is properly includable in=20
         intrinsic business value) far larger than the=20
         accounting Goodwill that is carried on our balance=20
         sheet and reflected in book value.

     Goodwill, both economic and accounting, is an arcane subject=20
and requires more explanation than is appropriate here.  The=20
appendix that follows this letter - =93Goodwill and its=20
Amortization: The Rules and The Realities=94 - explains why=20
economic and accounting Goodwill can, and usually do, differ=20
enormously.

     You can live a full and rewarding life without ever thinking=20
about Goodwill and its amortization.  But students of investment=20
and management should understand the nuances of the subject.  My=20
own thinking has changed drastically from 35 years ago when I was=20
taught to favor tangible assets and to shun businesses whose=20
value depended largely upon economic Goodwill.  This bias caused=20
me to make many important business mistakes of omission, although=20
relatively few of commission.

     Keynes identified my problem: =93The difficulty lies not in=20
the new ideas but in escaping from the old ones.=94 My escape was=20
long delayed, in part because most of what I had been taught by=20
the same teacher had been (and continues to be) so=20
extraordinarily valuable.  Ultimately, business experience,=20
direct and vicarious, produced my present strong preference for=20
businesses that possess large amounts of enduring Goodwill and=20
that utilize a minimum of tangible assets.

     I recommend the Appendix to those who are comfortable with=20
accounting terminology and who have an interest in understanding=20
the business aspects of Goodwill.  Whether or not you wish to=20
tackle the Appendix, you should be aware that Charlie and I=20
believe that Berkshire possesses very significant economic=20
Goodwill value above that reflected in our book value.

