<b>Stock Splits and Stock Activity</b>

     We often are asked why Berkshire does not split its stock. =20
The assumption behind this question usually appears to be that a=20
split would be a pro-shareholder action.  We disagree.  Let me=20
tell you why.

     One of our goals is to have Berkshire Hathaway stock sell at=20
a price rationally related to its intrinsic business value.  (But=20
note =93rationally related=94, not =93identical=94: if well-regarded=20
companies are generally selling in the market at large discounts=20
from value, Berkshire might well be priced similarly.) The key to=20
a rational stock price is rational shareholders, both current and=20
prospective.

     If the holders of a company=92s stock and/or the prospective=20
buyers attracted to it are prone to make irrational or emotion-
based decisions, some pretty silly stock prices are going to=20
appear periodically.  Manic-depressive personalities produce=20
manic-depressive valuations.  Such aberrations may help us in=20
buying and selling the stocks of other companies.  But we think=20
it is in both your interest and ours to minimize their occurrence=20
in the market for Berkshire.

     To obtain only high quality shareholders is no cinch.  Mrs.=20
Astor could select her 400, but anyone can buy any stock. =20
Entering members of a shareholder =93club=94 cannot be screened for=20
intellectual capacity, emotional stability, moral sensitivity or=20
acceptable dress.  Shareholder eugenics, therefore, might appear=20
to be a hopeless undertaking.

     In large part, however, we feel that high quality ownership=20
can be attracted and maintained if we consistently communicate=20
our business and ownership philosophy - <i>along with no other=20
conflicting messages</i> - and then let self selection follow its=20
course.  For example, self selection will draw a far different=20
crowd to a musical event advertised as an opera than one=20
advertised as a rock concert even though anyone can buy a ticket=20
to either.

     Through our policies and communications - our=20
=93advertisements=94 - we try to attract investors who will=20
understand our operations, attitudes and expectations. (And,=20
fully as important, we try to dissuade those who won=92t.) We want=20
those who think of themselves as business owners and invest in=20
companies with the intention of staying a long time.  And, we=20
want those who keep their eyes focused on business results, not=20
market prices.

     Investors possessing those characteristics are in a small=20
minority, but we have an exceptional collection of them.  I=20
believe well over 90% - probably over 95% - of our shares are=20
held by those who were shareholders of Berkshire or Blue Chip=20
five years ago.  And I would guess that over 95% of our shares=20
are held by investors for whom the holding is at least double the=20
size of their next largest.  Among companies with at least=20
several thousand public shareholders and more than $1 billion of=20
market value, we are almost certainly the leader in the degree to=20
which our shareholders think and act like owners.  Upgrading a=20
shareholder group that possesses these characteristics is not=20
easy.

     Were we to split the stock or take other actions focusing on=20
stock price rather than business value, we would attract an=20
entering class of buyers inferior to the exiting class of=20
sellers.  At $1300, there are very few investors who can=92t afford=20
a Berkshire share.  Would a potential one-share purchaser be=20
better off if we split 100 for 1 so he could buy 100 shares? =20
Those who think so and who would buy the stock because of the=20
split or in anticipation of one would definitely downgrade the=20
quality of our present shareholder group. (Could we really=20
improve our shareholder group by trading some of our present=20
clear-thinking members for impressionable new ones who,=20
preferring paper to value, feel wealthier with nine $10 bills=20
than with one $100 bill?) People who buy for non-value reasons=20
are likely to sell for non-value reasons.  Their presence in the=20
picture will accentuate erratic price swings unrelated to=20
underlying business developments.

     We will try to avoid policies that attract buyers with a=20
short-term focus on our stock price and try to follow policies=20
that attract informed long-term investors focusing on business=20
values. just as you purchased your Berkshire shares in a market=20
populated by rational informed investors, you deserve a chance to=20
sell - should you ever want to - in the same kind of market.  We=20
will work to keep it in existence.

     One of the ironies of the stock market is the emphasis on=20
activity.  Brokers, using terms such as =93marketability=94 and=20
=93liquidity=94, sing the praises of companies with high share=20
turnover (those who cannot fill your pocket will confidently fill=20
your ear).  But investors should understand that what is good for=20
the croupier is not good for the customer.  A hyperactive stock=20
market is the pickpocket of enterprise.

     For example, consider a typical company earning, say, 12% on=20
equity.  Assume a very high turnover rate in its shares of 100%=20
per year.  If a purchase and sale of the stock each extract=20
commissions of 1% (the rate may be much higher on low-priced=20
stocks) and if the stock trades at book value, the owners of our=20
hypothetical company will pay, in aggregate, 2% of the company=92s=20
net worth annually for the privilege of transferring ownership. =20
This activity does nothing for the earnings of the business, and=20
means that 1/6 of them are lost to the owners through the=20
=93frictional=94 cost of transfer. (And this calculation does not=20
count option trading, which would increase frictional costs still=20
further.)

     All that makes for a rather expensive game of musical=20
chairs.  Can you imagine the agonized cry that would arise if a=20
governmental unit were to impose a new 16 2/3% tax on earnings of=20
corporations or investors?  By market activity, investors can=20
impose upon themselves the equivalent of such a tax.

     Days when the market trades 100 million shares (and that=20
kind of volume, when over-the-counter trading is included, is=20
today abnormally low) are a curse for owners, not a blessing -=20
for they mean that owners are paying twice as much to change=20
chairs as they are on a 50-million-share day.  If 100 million-
share days persist for a year and the average cost on each=20
purchase and sale is 15 cents a share, the chair-changing tax for=20
investors in aggregate would total about $7.5 billion - an amount=20
roughly equal to the combined 1982 profits of Exxon, General=20
Motors, Mobil and Texaco, the four largest companies in the=20
Fortune 500.

     These companies had a combined net worth of $75 billion at=20
yearend 1982 and accounted for over 12% of both net worth and net=20
income of the entire Fortune 500 list.  Under our assumption=20
investors, in aggregate, every year forfeit all earnings from=20
this staggering sum of capital merely to satisfy their penchant=20
for =93financial flip-flopping=94.  In addition, investment=20
management fees of over $2 billion annually - sums paid for=20
chair-changing advice - require the forfeiture by investors of=20
all earnings of the five largest banking organizations (Citicorp,=20
Bank America, Chase Manhattan, Manufacturers Hanover and J. P.=20
Morgan).  These expensive activities may decide who eats the pie,=20
but they don=92t enlarge it.

     (We are aware of the pie-expanding argument that says that=20
such activities improve the rationality of the capital allocation=20
process.  We think that this argument is specious and that, on=20
balance, hyperactive equity markets subvert rational capital=20
allocation and act as pie shrinkers.  Adam Smith felt that all=20
noncollusive acts in a free market were guided by an invisible=20
hand that led an economy to maximum progress; our view is that=20
casino-type markets and hair-trigger investment management act as=20
an invisible foot that trips up and slows down a forward-moving=20
economy.)

     Contrast the hyperactive stock with Berkshire.  The bid-and-
ask spread in our stock currently is about 30 points, or a little=20
over 2%.  Depending on the size of the transaction, the=20
difference between proceeds received by the seller of Berkshire=20
and cost to the buyer may range downward from 4% (in trading=20
involving only a few shares) to perhaps 1 1/2% (in large trades=20
where negotiation can reduce both the market-maker=92s spread and=20
the broker=92s commission).  Because most Berkshire shares are=20
traded in fairly large transactions, the spread on all trading=20
probably does not average more than 2%.

     Meanwhile, true turnover in Berkshire stock (excluding=20
inter-dealer transactions, gifts and bequests) probably runs 3%=20
per year.  Thus our owners, in aggregate, are paying perhaps=20
6/100 of 1% of Berkshire=92s market value annually for transfer=20
privileges.  By this very rough estimate, that=92s $900,000 - not a=20
small cost, but far less than average.  Splitting the stock would=20
increase that cost, downgrade the quality of our shareholder=20
population, and encourage a market price less consistently=20
related to intrinsic business value.  We see no offsetting=20
advantages.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     Last year in this section I ran a small ad to encourage=20
acquisition candidates.  In our communications businesses we tell=20
our advertisers that repetition is a key to results (which it=20
is), so we will again repeat our acquisition criteria.

     We prefer:
        (1) large purchases (at least $5 million of after-tax=20
            earnings),
        (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
            projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
            =93turn-around=94 situations),
        (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
            employing little or no debt,
        (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),
        (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we=20
            won=92t understand it),
        (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time or=20
            that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
            about a transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we=92re interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuance of stock when we=20
receive as much in intrinsic business value as we give.  We=20
invite potential sellers to check us out by contacting people=20
with whom we have done business in the past.  For the right=20
business - and the right people - we can provide a good home.

                         *  *  *  *  *

     About 96.4% of all eligible shares participated in our 1983=20
shareholder-designated contributions program.  The total=20
contributions made pursuant to this program - disbursed in the=20
early days of 1984 but fully expensed in 1983 - were $3,066,501,=20
and 1353 charities were recipients.  Although the response=20
measured by the percentage of shares participating was=20
extraordinarily good, the response measured by the percentage of=20
holders participating was not as good.  The reason may well be=20
the large number of new shareholders acquired through the merger=20
and their lack of familiarity with the program.  We urge new=20
shareholders to read the description of the program on pages 52-
53.

     <i>If you wish to participate in future programs, we strongly=20
urge that you immediately make sure that your shares are=20
registered in the actual owner=92s name, not in =93street=94 or nominee=20
name.  Shares not so registered on September 28, 1984 will not be=20
eligible for any 1984 program.</i>

                         *  *  *  *  *

     The Blue Chip/Berkshire merger went off without a hitch. =20
Less than one-tenth of 1% of the shares of each company voted=20
against the merger, and no requests for appraisal were made.  In=20
1983, we gained some tax efficiency from the merger and we expect=20
to gain more in the future.

     One interesting sidelight to the merger: Berkshire now has=20
1,146,909 shares outstanding compared to 1,137,778 shares at the=20
beginning of fiscal 1965, the year present management assumed=20
responsibility.  For every 1% of the company you owned at that=20
time, you now would own .99%. Thus, all of today=92s assets - the=20
News, See=92s, Nebraska Furniture Mart, the Insurance Group, $1.3=20
billion in marketable stocks, etc. - have been added to the=20
original textile assets with virtually no net dilution to the=20
original owners.

     We are delighted to have the former Blue Chip shareholders=20
join us.  To aid in your understanding of Berkshire Hathaway, we=20
will be glad to send you the Compendium of Letters from the=20
Annual Reports of 1977-1981, and/or the 1982 Annual report. =20
Direct your request to the Company at 1440 Kiewit Plaza, Omaha,=20
